Creates a Spacelift stack that runs a single Ansible playbook from the
`ansible-playbooks` repository against Azure Government.

## Before you deploy

Make sure the space you're deploying into matches your team. The service
principal your playbook authenticates with is scoped to the space, so
deploying into the wrong one will fail at Azure login even though the
stack itself is created successfully.

## Inputs

**Playbook** — the playbook this stack runs. One stack runs one playbook.
If you need three playbooks, deploy this template three times.

**Environment** — sets the target subscription and the deploy behaviour:

| Environment | Runs deploy automatically? |
|---|---|
| dev  | Yes, on every merge to `main` |
| test | No, someone confirms each run |
| prod | No, someone confirms each run |

**Stack identifier** — a short name describing what this stack does, not
which playbook it runs. Lowercase letters, numbers and hyphens only.
The full stack name is generated as `ansible-<identifier>-<environment>`,
so `web-tier` in dev becomes `ansible-web-tier-dev`.

## After you deploy

The stack appears immediately but does not run until the next push to
`main`, or until you trigger a run manually from the stack page.

Runner image, Azure credentials and worker pool are all managed centrally.
You don't need to configure them, and they aren't editable on the created
stack — that's intentional. If you need something changed, it happens
through this template.

## Getting help

Post in #platform-engineering. Include the deployment name and the run URL.
